  "models": {
    "simulation-2008-01": "Nobody has imaged this corona. This is the gas density in the magnetohydrodynamic simulation Ó Fionnagáin, Kavanagh, Vidotto et al. (2022), ApJ 924, 115 ran for this star, driven by the map of its surface magnetic field observed in January 2008 (Jeffers et al. (2014), A&A 569, A79), from the data they released (https://zenodo.org/records/5575241). The released state is ten minutes after the authors set off an eruption near the equator of the model; that eruption disturbs 6% of the volume drawn here. It is shown the way coronagraph pictures are: at each distance from the star brightness is proportional to the density there, and only the steep fall-off with distance is compressed. The rotation axis is tilted 46° from the line of sight, as the magnetic maps were fitted; these sources give neither where the axis points on the sky nor the star's rotation phase today, so both are drawn by convention.",
    "simulation-2011-10": "Nobody has imaged this corona. This is the gas density in the magnetohydrodynamic simulation Ó Fionnagáin, Kavanagh, Vidotto et al. (2022), ApJ 924, 115 ran for this star, driven by the map of its surface magnetic field observed in October 2011 (Jeffers et al. (2014), A&A 569, A79), from the data they released (https://zenodo.org/records/5575241). The released state is ten minutes after the authors set off an eruption near the equator of the model; that eruption disturbs 9% of the volume drawn here. It is shown the way coronagraph pictures are: at each distance from the star brightness is proportional to the density there, and only the steep fall-off with distance is compressed. The rotation axis is tilted 46° from the line of sight, as the magnetic maps were fitted; these sources give neither where the axis points on the sky nor the star's rotation phase today, so both are drawn by convention.",
    "simulation-2013-10": "Nobody has imaged this corona. This is the gas density in the magnetohydrodynamic simulation Ó Fionnagáin, Kavanagh, Vidotto et al. (2022), ApJ 924, 115 ran for this star, driven by the map of its surface magnetic field observed in October 2013 (Jeffers et al. (2014), A&A 569, A79), from the data they released (https://zenodo.org/records/5575241). The released state is ten minutes after the authors set off an eruption near the equator of the model; that eruption disturbs 4% of the volume drawn here. It is shown the way coronagraph pictures are: at each distance from the star brightness is proportional to the density there, and only the steep fall-off with distance is compressed. The rotation axis is tilted 46° from the line of sight, as the magnetic maps were fitted; these sources give neither where the axis points on the sky nor the star's rotation phase today, so both are drawn by convention.",
    "wind": "Nobody has imaged this corona. This is the density of the wind Parker (1958) derives for gas at 4.06 million kelvin, the temperature measured from the star's X-ray spectrum, carrying 30 times the Sun's mass loss, which Wood et al. (2002) measure from the star's astrosphere. It leaves the surface at 93 kilometres a second and passes the speed of sound 1.89 radii out. It is the same in every direction, because the model has no magnetic field; the simulated datasets show what the field does to it."
  },
  "limitations": [
    "No telescope has imaged this corona; every grid is a simulation or a model.",
    "Each simulated grid is an instant ten minutes after an eruption the authors set off; the steady state it started from was not released. The drawn run is the one whose eruption disturbs the least of the drawn volume.",
    "The simulation resolves the magnetic field only as far as the maps do: the large-scale field, not active regions.",
    "The rotation axis is tilted from the line of sight by the fitted inclination; its position angle on the sky and the rotation phase are conventions, not measurements.",
    "The magnetic maps are from 2008, 2011 and 2013; the star’s field changes within months.",
    "The wind grid assumes one temperature at every distance and the same outflow in every direction.",
    "The radial filter, the logarithmic scale, the two radial fades and the exposure are display choices."
  ]
}
